   },
   {
    "op": "replace",
    "find": " — and the choice of tense — changes the meaning of the sentence entirely.",
    "replace": " to an obligation is non-standard, while plain deber is also widely accepted for a guess (debe ser tarde), although deber de is the preferred form for probability. The choice of tense matters too: deberías gives advice.",
    "finding": "deber-de framing"
   },
   {
    "op": "replace",
    "find": "In informal spoken Spanish, deber and deber de are sometimes mixed. In formal writing and in exams, the distinction above is expected and should be maintained.",
    "replace": "Only one direction is a real mistake: deber de for obligation (debes de terminarlo) is non-standard. Deber without de for a guess (deben ser las cinco) is so widespread that the RAE accepts it, though deber de is preferred. In formal writing and in exams, keep deber for obligation and deber de for probability.",
    "finding": "deber-de framing"
   },
   {
    "op": "set",
    "path": "/sections/4/body/0/items/1/0",
    "value": {
     "kind": "paragraph",
     "text": [
      {
       "kind": "strong",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "Treating deber and deber de as fully interchangeable"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": ": The overlap only goes one way. Plain deber can also express a guess — «Deben ser las cinco» (it must be five o'clock) is widely used and accepted, although deber de is preferred. But deber de cannot replace deber for obligation: «deben terminar» (they must finish) states a duty, while «deben de terminar» can only be a guess (they probably finish)."
      }
     ]
    },
    "finding": "deber-de framing"
   }
  ]
